\subsection{PPCI Complementarity and Estimator Robustness}
\label{sec:ppci}

EC-HTE composes with PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} for settings where both covariate misclassification and outcome model error are present: EC-HTE+PPCI reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.179 to 0.007 ($z{=}0$, DGP~C), and the corrected pseudo-outcomes are compatible with other CATE estimators (R-learner bias reduction: 89\%; Appendix~\ref{app:ppci},~\ref{app:rlearner}).

All six models exhibit effective $\Delta\pi > 0.05$ on the four retained subgroups (Table~\ref{tab:multilm}), and 5 of 6 share the same worst subgroup (\texttt{True\_medium}: tweets with hashtags and medium text length).
Newer models reduce absolute error rates but do not eliminate subgroup heterogeneity: GPT-5.4 achieves $\Delta\pi = 0.114$ compared to GPT-3.5-Turbo's $0.198$.
These results were obtained with a single API provider; cross-provider validation is left to future work.

\section{Robustness to Subgroup Misspecification}
\label{app:robustness}

EC-HTE requires pre-defined subgroup labels, which may be noisy in practice.
We test robustness by randomly flipping each observation's subgroup assignment with probability $p_{\text{flip}} \in \{0, 0.10, 0.25, 0.50\}$, across $K \in \{2, 4\}$ (extreme regime, $n_{\text{expert}} = 500$, $\Delta\tau = 0.3$, 100 MC runs).

